<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marthanda Varma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1706">1706</a>–<a href="/years/1758">1758</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marthanda Varma was ruler of Venad from 1729 who built it into the kingdom of Travancore at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He crushed the power of local nobles, annexed neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> such as Kayamkulam, and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> at Colachel in 1741. In 1750 he dedicated his kingdom to the god Padmanabha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a>, ruling thereafter as the deity's servant.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma.jpg" alt="Marthanda Varma" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anizham_Thirunal_Marthanda_Varma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marthanda Varma was born in 1706 to Karthika Thirunal Umadevi, senior queen of Attingal, and Raghava Varma of Kilimanoor Palace, who died when the boy was one year old. He was the nephew of the Venadu king Rama Varma, whom he was to succeed under the matrilineal inheritance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. When Rama Varma died in 1729, his sons Valiya Thampi and Kunju Thampi claimed the throne, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> aristocracy of the Ettuveettil Pillamar and their associates the Yogakkar, who held more power than the kings. Many of Marthanda Varma's relatives were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He killed the Thampis, used spies against the Pillamar, executed most of their families and destroyed the power of more than 70 nobles, expelling the Yogakkar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> who had supported them. He built a standing army of about 50,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> men, used Marava and Pathan cavalry, and fortified the north with the Travancore Lines. Between 1743, when he declared a state monopoly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, and 1752 he annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Quilon</a>, Kayamkulam, Thekkumkur, Vadakkumkur and Purakkad, aiming at the spice lands that supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>.</p><p>After the victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at Colachel on 10 August 1741 he took Eustachius de Lannoy, one of the prisoners, into his service; de Lannoy drilled a regiment and rose to general. The Treaty of Mavelikkara with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> followed on 15 August 1753. A Dalava headed military, fiscal and commercial administration over a new hierarchy of officials, black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and other goods became royal monopolies, and Syrian Christian traders received his patronage as a check on European trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He rebuilt the Padmanabhaswamy Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a> on its present scale, introduced state ceremonies such as Murajapam and Bhadra Deepam, and patronised Kathakali, Koodiyattam and Thullal; Kunchan Nambiar was among his court poets. He died in 1758, two years after his friend and minister Ramayyan Dalawa. His nephew Rama Varma, Dharma Raja, continued his policies and defended Travancore against the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marthanda Varma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chera empire had dissolved by about 1100 and the region split into many small kingdoms until <b>Marthanda Varma</b>, king of Venad from 1729, unified them by brutal methods; in his reign Thiruvithamkoor became the official name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1750 <b>Marthanda Varma</b>, king of Travancore in southern India, gave his kingdom to the god Padmanabha, a form of Vishnu; he and his successors then ruled as the deity’s servants, with the title Padmanabhadasa.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch power was checked when <b>Marthanda Varma</b> of Travancore defeated them at Colachel in 1741.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-malabar">Dutch Malabar</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1720s and 1730s <b>Marthanda Varma</b> crushed the nobles, absorbed neighbouring chiefdoms and transformed Venad into Travancore, the register's successor state from 1729.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-venad">Kingdom of Venad</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Attingal, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Padmanabhapuram, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=8.7,76.81&amp;year=1739" aria-label="Attingal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Marthanda Varma"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3895 4259h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3895 4259h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=8.7,76.81&amp;year=1739">What was here in 1739? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
